--TEST--
`Core\Command::run` refuses a word its parameter's union does not declare, and says every word that would have been accepted — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The other side of `command-run-converts-a-set-of-allowed-values-argument-by-its-word`,
// and the half § 6 argues the conversion's *shape* from: a command line is a
// person typing, so a refusal is worth raising only if it says what to type
// instead. That is why `nvs_types::commands::ArgConv::OneOf` carries the words
// themselves rather than the type they came from.
//
// Read off standard error, where a usage page goes, because § 6's status alone
// would pass for a refusal that named nothing — and the refusal naming the set
// is the whole decision. `%A` swallows the usage page under it: what this case
// owns is the sentence, and the page's layout is `nvs_stdlib::command`'s.
//
// `exit` carries the status out, because a case that reads standard error must
// end in failure — `crates/nvs-test`'s own rule, so that a case cannot pass by
// printing the right thing on its way to an unexpected one.
class Reports {
    #[Core\Command(name: "report", about: "Write one report")]
    public static function run("json"|"table" $format): uint {
        echo "format: ", $format, "\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

uint $status = Core\Command::run();
echo "status: ", $status, "\n";
exit($status as int);
--ARGS--
report
csv
--EXPECT--
status: 2
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
`format` is one of `json`, `table`, and `csv` is none of them
%A
